  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1239 PM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nTuesday-Wednesday.\n\nApproaching midweek, the upper high will slowly shift east as a \nbroad trough passes by to our north. That being said, the downtick \nin expected activity has continued with the latest batch of model \nruns. A now-dry cold front will move onto the plains fairly early in \nthe day, which will significantly lower temperatures across the \nregion. Highs will only be in the 80s to low-90s for most areas. \nOver the mountains, post-frontal upsloping will combine with diurnal \nheating, along with some synoptic forcing from a passing shortwave \nto allow for showers and thunderstorms through the late afternoon on \nTuesday. Main area of focus will be along the southeast mountains, \nfollowed by portions of the Continental Divide. Once again, the \nAspen Acres burn scar will be a primary concern when looking at \nflash flooding potential.\n\nFor Wednesday, the shortwave will quickly push off to the southeast, \ntaking most of the activity along with it. As of now, only isolated \nafternoon showers are expected over the mountains and I-25, with \nmore scattered showers and a few thunderstorms over the Raton Mesa, \nsouthern Sangres, and portions of the southeast plains. High \ntemperatures will warm a few degrees on Wednesday as well, but will \nstill remain at or below the mid-90s.\n\nThursday Onwards...\n\nThrough the end of this week, the upper high builds back in over the \nDesert Southwest. As a result, our region will largely dry out, \nthough some isolated afternoon convection will still be possible, \nmainly over our southern mountains. Coverage may manage to increase \na bit if any embedded energy travels around the center of the upper \nhigh, but as of now model solutions continue to trend towards this \ndrier solution. Temperatures will also continue to rebound, \nincreasing back into the triple digits out east by the end of the \nweekend.",
